  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T06:08:53.109Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nAn individual earning P250,000 gross compensation income is not required to file an annual income tax return, provided that the income tax has been correctly withheld by the employer.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThis is governed by Section 51-A of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from only one employer, where the tax due equals the tax withheld, are exempt from filing an annual income tax return.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51-A of the NIRC allows for substituted filing of income tax returns for employees whose income tax has been withheld correctly by their employer. Since the individual in question has a gross compensation income of P250,000, which falls under the threshold where no tax is due (0% tax rate), they are not required to file a return if the employer has withheld the correct amount.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practical terms, this means that if the individual’s employer has withheld the appropriate taxes from their compensation, they can rely on the employer's withholding certificate as sufficient documentation for tax purposes, thus simplifying their tax obligations. This provision is beneficial for employees as it reduces the administrative burden of filing a return when their tax situation is straightforward.",
